pr_comments:
Question to reviewers: is it better to make a flag part of the `k_work_queue_config` struct ?
```
struct k_work_queue_config {
	const char *name;
	bool no_yield;
	bool essential;
};
```

so it can be passed to `k_work_queue_start` ?

review_comments:
> instead of making essential a global config for all work queues, it makes more sense to have a 'system' config, indicating this is a system work queue, then based on this, you can set the essential flag on system work queues.

you mean to have something like:
```c
/* new type in addition to `k_work_queue_config` */
struct k_sys_work_queue_config {
	const char *name;
	bool no_yield;
	bool essential;
};

void k_sys_work_queue_start(struct k_work_q *sys_queue,
			k_thread_stack_t *stack,
			size_t stack_size,
			int prio,
			const struct k_sys_work_queue_config *cfg)
{
	struct k_work_queue_config cfg = {
		.name = cfg->name,
		.no_yield = cfg->no_yield
	};
	k_work_queue_start(sys_queue, stack, stack_size, prio)
	sys_queue.thread.base.user_options |= K_ESSENTIAL;
}
```



But I would like to say that the idea behind including `essential` in the queue config is to allow the user to mark their own queue also as essential not only system work queue. for example we have a system where it uses a custom Bluetooth transport which has its own workq and if this workq thread crashed it doesn't make sense for our system to continue to work. WDYT ?

